    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung</a> kings united the Tibetan plateau in the 7th century and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> as divine emperors (tsenpo).</li><li>Its cavalry fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, and briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 763.</li><li>It gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> its script and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the religion of the court, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a> monastery around 779.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The peace treaty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> swore with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 821–822 was carved in Tibetan and Chinese on a stone pillar that still stands in front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

