    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo.jpg" alt="Songtsen Gampo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_King_Songtsen_Gampo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bhavna Sayana, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tibetan Empire grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung</a> valley in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, where a line of chiefs later remembered as the first kings of Tibet held lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo">Yarlung Tsangpo</a> river. In the late sixth and early seventh centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namri-songtsen">Namri Songtsen</a>, allied with clan leaders who had deserted rival lords, extended Yarlung authority over much of central Tibet. The chronology of this period is uncertain, and the year 618 used here is conventional. Namri Songtsen was poisoned, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, who probably came to the throne as a youth around 618 or somewhat later, suppressed revolts by the conquered clans and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> his seat. He brought the Sumpa of the north-east under his rule and, in the 640s, absorbed the kingdom of Zhangzhung in western Tibet, partly through a marriage alliance and partly by force. In 634 he sent his first embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, and after a Tibetan attack on the Tang frontier at Songzhou in 638 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Taizong</a> agreed to a marriage alliance: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-wencheng">Princess Wencheng</a> arrived in Tibet in 641. Tibetan tradition adds a Nepalese wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a>, whose historicity is debated. When Songtsen Gampo died around 649, Tibet was a single kingdom stretching across much of the plateau and recognised by its neighbours as a major power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunhuang.jpg" alt="Dunhuang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiucenglou_of_Mogao_Caves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">慕尼黑啤酒, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> was a society of aristocratic clans, free commoners and dependent households bound to estates. Great families such as the Gar, Nanam, Dro, Tsepong and Chims held land, supplied ministers and royal consorts, and competed for influence at court. The economy rested on barley farming in the river valleys of central Tibet and on the herding of yaks, sheep, goats and horses on the high pastures, and the state recorded both through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> and tax registers. Conquest added wealth: tribute from the Tuyuhun, Nanzhao and the oasis cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and from the 760s the farmland and trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. Tibet exported horses, musk and gold, and its control of routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other luxuries to the court. Religion combined the cult of the divine king with rites performed by priests whom later sources call Bon, including elaborate royal funerals in the tombs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongye">Chongye</a> valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> arrived from China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and India, first through royal temples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, traditionally founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>'s reign, and later through monasteries endowed with land and households. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> from Dunhuang show officials, soldiers and monks writing contracts, letters and prayers in Tibetan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Dafei River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river">Battle of Dafei River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Dafei_River.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire's strength lay in its army, raised through military districts called 'thousand-districts' (<em>stong sde</em>), grouped into large regional divisions known as 'horns' (<em>ru</em>), each district owing a fixed number of soldiers. Tibetan cavalry, protected by chain mail that Chinese observers described with respect, fought alongside levies from conquered peoples. Under the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-tongtsen">Gar Tongtsen</a> and his sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> conquered the Tuyuhun around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Lake Kokonor</a> in 663, and in 670 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-trinring-tsendro">Gar Trinring Tsendro</a> defeated a Tang army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengui">Xue Rengui</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river">Dafei River</a>. In the same years Tibetan forces with Western Turkic allies overran the Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, which the Tang retook in 692. Rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the Turks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a>, where the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-xianzhi">Gao Xianzhi</a>'s expedition of 747 seized Little Balur, a Tibetan ally. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> (755–763) opened the way to the greatest Tibetan victories: in 763 an army briefly occupied the Tang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and installed a puppet emperor, and over the next two decades Tibet took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> around 786. From 752 the kingdom of Nanzhao in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> was a Tibetan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and ally, but in 794 it changed sides and inflicted a heavy defeat on Tibetan forces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota.jpg" alt="Thonmi Sambhota" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota">Thonmi Sambhota</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomni-sambhota-thangka-72-for-web-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sahil Bhopal, Tashi Mannox, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the head of the state stood the emperor, the <em>tsenpo</em>, regarded as a divine son descended from the heavens, whose person bound the clans together. In practice power was shared with a council of ministers led by a great minister (<em>blon chen</em>), drawn from the leading clans. For long periods the throne was overshadowed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-clan">Gar clan</a> dominated government after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>'s death until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tridu-songtsen">Tridu Songtsen</a> destroyed it in 698, and several emperors came to the throne as children under regents and powerful queen mothers. Royal assemblies, held each summer and winter, settled matters of taxation, conscription and law, and are recorded year by year in the <em>Old Tibetan Annals</em>, which begin around 650. The state conducted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>, kept land and tax registers and issued edicts, some of them inscribed on stone pillars such as the Zhol pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, which records the services of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takdra-lukhong">Takdra Lukhong</a>. Writing was central to this administration: a script derived from Indian models, attributed by tradition to the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota">Thonmi Sambhota</a> in Songtsen Gampo's reign, was used for laws, correspondence and military orders. Conquered regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> were organised into military districts staffed by Tibetan and local officials, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers kept their thrones in return for tribute and troops.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samye"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samye.jpg" alt="Samye" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_grand_view_of_Samye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tibetan Empire shaped the political map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a> for two centuries. Its wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, fought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, drained Tang resources, and its occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s direct overland link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for some eighty years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> also became, for the first time, a single cultural sphere. The Tibetan script, still in use today, and a standardised written language spread across the plateau and into conquered territories, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them the <em>Old Tibetan Annals</em> and the <em>Old Tibetan Chronicle</em>, form the oldest substantial body of historical writing in Tibetan. Royal patronage made Tibet a Buddhist land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> invited the Indian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shantarakshita">Shantarakshita</a> and, by tradition, the tantric master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a> monastery around 779 and, according to later accounts, presided over a debate around 792–794 in which Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> prevailed over Chinese Chan teaching. In the early ninth century translators standardised the terms used to render <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts, compiling the lexicon known as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavyutpatti">Mahavyutpatti</a></em>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> remembered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, Trisong Detsen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralpachen">Ralpachen</a> as the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> kings, and the memory of the empire underpinned the legitimacy of later Tibetan rulers and lineages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ralpachen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ralpachen.jpg" alt="Ralpachen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ralpachen">Ralpachen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralpachen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the early ninth century the empire's resources were stretched by wars on several fronts and by the cost of supporting the Buddhist establishment, which had gained tax-exempt estates and growing influence at court. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralpachen">Ralpachen</a> (r. 815–838) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> made peace with its main rivals: a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> was sworn in 821–822, and its text, in Tibetan and Chinese, was carved on a pillar that still stands before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>; a settlement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> was reached at about the same time. Ralpachen, a lavish patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, was murdered in 838, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Udumtsen</a>, known to later tradition as Langdarma, succeeded him. Later Buddhist histories portray Langdarma as a persecutor of the faith who was assassinated by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhalung-palgyi-dorje">Lhalung Palgyi Dorje</a>, traditionally in 842; modern historians accept that he withdrew state support from the monasteries and was killed, but treat the details with caution. His death left two claimants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osung">Ösung</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yumten">Yumten</a>, backed by rival factions, and central government collapsed. Tibetan commanders in the north-east fought one another, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> was retaken by the local leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yichao">Zhang Yichao</a> in 848, and revolts broke out in central Tibet from 869. The plateau entered the long Era of Fragmentation, while descendants of the royal line founded small kingdoms such as Guge in western Tibet.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Tibetan Empire united the Tibetan plateau for the first time and for two centuries contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the Turks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, while its kings gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> a script, a written language and the royal patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> that came to define Tibetan civilisation.</em></p>

